175: The product of its four multipliers is one, so it fixes the
176: base monomial \(q=z_0z_1z_2z_4\).  Its torus vector is
177: \(\log a\,(u_1-u_0)+\log(br^{2mn})\,(u_2-u_0)\),
178: which is exactly the marked \((Z,V)\) action.  Thus its
179: parameter-dependent factor is one global physical delta
180: action; it is not two separately chosen gauges at the two
181: ends of the exceptional geometry.
182: 
183: For the exact quotient, label the unit-square flop cells by
184: \(v\in\mathbb Z^2\).  Their period lattice is
185: \(\Gamma=\langle(2,1),(-3,-1)\rangle=\mathbb Z^2\),
186: and \(3(2,1)+2(-3,-1)=(0,1)=K_{\rm trop}\).
187: It acts freely and transitively on these cells.  Their diagonal
188: curves are disjoint, since each has the two maximal cones
189: belonging to its own square.  Choose disjoint equivariant
190: tubular neighbourhoods and insert one master chart in each.
191: The quotient of their disjoint union by \(\Gamma\) is
192: exactly one representative chart with trivial stabilizer.
193: There is no residual \(K\)-circle on this correction.
194: The signed parameter return shifts its cell label by
195: \(n(0,1)=n(3h+2g)\).  Returning to the chosen representative
196: by that unique period gives (P3K.17.3).  Reversing the
197: representative convention inverts the one common gauge at
198: both ends and leaves the comparison unchanged.
199: 
200: Fixing \(|r|\), its angular part is
201: \(e^{2mn i\theta}\) in that physical circle.  The remaining
202: unit multipliers are independent of \(\theta\); their
203: chosen logarithms give equivariant isotopies on the Cox
204: charts.  The positive real rescalings in Section 1 transport
205: these isotopies to the moment levels and to the inner face.
206: Explicitly, follow a Cox map by its unique positive auxiliary
207: rescaling back to the original value of \(\mu\).  Its
208: implicit derivative is
209: \(2r^2\|a\|^2+2r^{-2}\|c\|^2>0\) away from the
210: removed origin, so this is smooth through the real bordism.
211: The bounded units can be changed to their values at \(q=0\)
212: using \(a(tq),b(tq)\), \(0\le t\le1\), with an
213: exterior cutoff.  The maps stay invertible and commute with
214: both circles; they are independent of the parameter angle.
215: This change introduces no angular character on the inner face.
216: These maps need not preserve a fixed Euclidean radius.
217: Use transported truncations: under the moment-preserving
218: equivariant isotopy \(G_t\), take the inner and outer
219: faces to be \(G_t\) of the original faces.  They stay
220: embedded and disjoint, while the moment faces stay at
221: \(\mu=\pm\epsilon\).  Glue these transported charts
222: and collars by their exact maps.  For the remaining
223: constant-unit clutch \(G_0\), its chosen logarithms give
224: \(G_0^t\), and
225: \((x,t)\mapsto(G_0^t x,e^{2\pi it})\) trivializes
226: the mapping torus.  The angular delta factor preserves
227: the round face and is trivial on its circle orbit.
228: Consequently the two maps (P3K.17.2) have the same angular
229: gauge, which extends by that constant circle multiplication
230: over all of \(C\times S^2_Q\).  No difference of two
231: angular gauges is introduced on its ends.  The closed
232: correction over the parameter circle therefore carries
233: a line pulled back from \(Q_{\rm corr}\), after this
234: explicit gauge identification.  Its first Pontryagin class
235: is pulled back from the same five-dimensional factor.
236: The potential mixed line class can also be checked directly.
237: Cover the correction by its free-orbit cylinder and joint-cap
238: cylinder; their overlap has two components.  The actual
239: transition functions there are
240: \(\varphi_1(\theta)=g(\theta)\varphi_{1,0}\) and
241: \(\varphi_2(\theta)=g(\theta)\varphi_{2,0}\), with
242: the same scalar from (P3K.17.3).  Change the cap frame by
243: \(g(\theta)^{-1}\).  Both angular factors disappear
244: simultaneously, leaving parameter-independent transitions.
245: This explicitly excludes a line class on
246: \(S^1_{\rm joint}\times S^1_{\rm parameter}\).
247: Both six-dimensional characteristic numbers vanish:
248: \[
249:   \langle x^3,[Q_{\rm corr}\times S^1]\rangle=0,
250:   \qquad
251:   \langle p_1x,[Q_{\rm corr}\times S^1]\rangle=0.       \tag{P3K.17.4}
252: \]
253: 
254: **Proof: the additional normal-sphere boundary in the surgery bordism.**
255: 
256: The fixed locus in the master bordism must also be retained.
257: There are exactly two relevant auxiliary-quotient fixed charts:
258: \((z_4,z_1)\) and \((z_0,z_2)\).  Each has auxiliary
259: weights \((1,-1)\), and its quotient is \(\mathbb R^3\)
260: by the explicit invariant map
261: \[
262:       (z_+,z_-)\longmapsto
263:        \bigl(2\Re(z_+z_-),2\Im(z_+z_-),
264:                          |z_+|^2-|z_-|^2\bigr).
265: \]
266: The inverse orbit is determined by
267: \(|z_\pm|^2=(\sqrt{\mu^2+4|z_+z_-|^2}\pm\mu)/2\).
268: The two moment faces in each chart are discs.  Removing the
269: inner master-space ball removes an interior three-ball in
270: each of these fixed charts.  Away from the flop curve the
271: fixed sphere minus its two endpoint discs is unchanged.
272: Gluing this annulus times the moment interval proves that
273: the fixed three-dimensional cobordism is exactly
274: \[
275:                  W_F=(S^2\times I)\setminus
276:                              (\mathring D^3\sqcup\mathring D^3).
277: \]
278: The Cox reindexing preserves the two charts; it does not
279: permute or identify their boundary spheres.
280: 
281: The oriented normal three-plane bundle is trivial over this
282: three-dimensional cobordism, with its boundary product
283: structures retained.  On the two Cox fixed charts its
284: positive-weight complex normal bundle is respectively
285: \(L^{\oplus2}\) and \((L^{-1})^{\oplus2}\), so the
286: associated three-plane bundle has \(w_2=0\).  The other
287: two boundary spheres have the original \(SU(2)\) normal
288: model.  Restriction of \(H^2(W_F;\mathbb Z/2)\) to
289: the four boundary spheres is injective, so \(w_2\) vanishes
290: globally.  An oriented three-plane bundle on a three-dimensional
291: complex with this class zero is trivial: the obstruction on
292: two-cells is \(\pi_1(SO(3))=\mathbb Z/2\), and that
293: on three-cells is \(\pi_2(SO(3))=0\).  Boundary
294: trivializations differ by maps \(S^2\to SO(3)\), all
295: null-homotopic.  Collar homotopies therefore match the chosen
296: product trivializations at every boundary.  The actual circle
297: line restricted to the constant normal section has boundary
298: values \(0,0,+h_A,-h_B\).
299: 
300: Cap the two original-family sphere boundaries by \(D^3\),
301: as in their actual conifold construction, and join the two
302: inner-face spheres by the charged cap \(C\) above.  This
303: closes \(W_F\) to \(S^2\times S^1\).  Crossing with
304: the parameter circle closes the fixed four-dimensional base
305: to \(\bar X\).  The described local trivialization makes
306: it the product with that parameter circle, retaining the
307: possible parameter-independent clutch along the first
308: \(S^1\).  In particular it is a mapping torus over the
309: first circle of \(S^2\), crossed with the parameter circle.
310: All gluings preserve the sphere orientation.  Its signature
311: is zero, and its tangent first Pontryagin number is zero.
312: For the exact orientation comparison, capping the first two
313: boundaries leaves \(S^2\times I\).  Its line has degrees
314: \(+1\) and \(-1\) in the two complex boundary markings.
315: Its product transport consequently has degree \(-1\)
316: between those markings.  The joint cap has exactly that
317: antipodal base change at its second end.  The composite has
318: degree \(+1\), giving the oriented sphere mapping torus
319: above.  On the meridian sphere the first end map is also
320: antipodal, so both meridian orientations give the same
321: physical Euler value one.
322: 
323: Apply the explicit collar construction of P3K.7 to the
324: complement of this fixed locus in the master-space orbit
325: family.  At the two original-family boundaries it gives
326: their actual conifold mapping tori.  At the inner face it
327: gives \(Q_{\rm corr}\times S^1\).  It has one further
328: boundary, the normal sphere bundle \(B=S(\nu_{\bar X})\).
329: The meridian-one line is glued by (P3K.17.2) and (P3K.17.3)
330: through every collar, so it extends across this oriented
331: bordism.  This accounts for the boundary which would be
332: missed by capping the two inner fixed spheres separately.
333: 
334: The actual parameter-dependent Cox action is delta, which
335: acts trivially on the circle orbit and its normal rank-three
336: bundle.  Thus on the closed fixed base both its normal data
337: and the line data are pulled back from the three-dimensional
338: \(S^2\)-mapping-torus factor.  With the notation of P3K.7,
339: \(e_{\mathrm{vert}}=2x-\pi^*b\), it follows that
340: \(b^2=0\).  The sphere-bundle calculation, valid as an
341: oriented characteristic calculation, gives
342: \[
343:        \frac{4\langle x^3,[B]\rangle
344:                   -\langle p_1(TB)x,[B]\rangle}{24}
345:        =\frac{3\langle b^2,[\bar X]\rangle
346:                     -\langle p_1(T\bar X),[\bar X]\rangle}{24}
347:        =0.                                             \tag{P3K.17.5}
348: \]
349: No value of \(p_1(\nu_{\bar X})\) is assumed here.
350: 
351: For a closed oriented six-manifold with a complex line,
352: \(4x^3-p_1x\) is an oriented characteristic number and
353: vanishes on oriented boundaries carrying that line.  Hence
354: the two original-family return values are equal after
355: (P3K.17.4) and (P3K.17.5).  The actual endpoint manifolds
356: already have their prescribed spin structures, so their
357: integer \(A\) is the same characteristic expression.
358: The signs of the two additional boundary components do not
359: affect their zero contributions; the two moment faces have
360: opposite boundary orientations.  Together with P3F.32 this identifies the negative-diagonal
361: return index with zero for all the stated orders and twists.
362: The exact Hopf transfer P3F.22 gives its oriented zero class,
363: and P3F.24 gives the product mapping torus.
364: 
365: **End of proof.**
366: 
367: ## Audit record
368: 
369: The initial draft and its explicit audit boundary are preserved in
370: `code/part3_fourfold/history/part3_flop_cobordism_audit.md`.
371: Independent blind-control audit checked the residual semifree weights,
372: both associated-line signs, the common Cox delta gauge, and the extra
373: normal-sphere term.  Root audit additionally required and then accepted
374: the transported truncations and the free period-orbit quotient before
375: the two-overlap Cech gauge cancellation.  The orientation transport
376: between the two complex fixed-sphere markings was explicitly retained.
377: The exact matrix and gauge certificate is `code/part3_fourfold/cox_flop.json`.
378: This proof treats the two stated OS49 diagonal fans; it does not assign
379: the same quotient model to every periodic unimodular fan.
